{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgw7APjEogU7jNbVDVdZ7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Wm5Jw73sJiLTwtGC327iEs","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; for k in Willett2013 Liu2024 Fannjiang Huang2025 Gan2022 Tran2025 Khelfaoui Zobeiri; do echo $k $(grep -c $k paper.tex); done; grep -n -iE 'plankton|deforest|gene expression|protein disorder|galaxy' paper.tex | grep -E 'cite' ; sed -n 300,330p paper.tex; grep -iE 'plankton|deforest|expression|disorder|galaxy|isotonic' paper_strategy.json claims_grounding.json | head -20; sed -n 1,25p VENUE.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16353,"cache_read_input_tokens":17828,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16353},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c3f5a306-5336-48e1-ab3a-94ad94a1a36a","uuid":"d7f7e5ae-9572-4e80-a9e5-cf4c1b398719","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:02:54.911Z","request_id":"req_011Cgw7APV6hGdQE1cS6jzyP"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wm5Jw73sJiLTwtGC327iEs","type":"tool_result","content":"Willett2013 1\nLiu2024 0\nFannjiang 0\nHuang2025 0\nGan2022 0\nTran2025 0\nKhelfaoui 0\nZobeiri 0\n44:In ecological remote sensing, galaxy morphology and census analysis, a scientist often holds three things: a small set of gold-standard labels, a large set of records without labels, and a pretrained predictor that cannot be modified. Prediction-powered inference \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PredictionpoweredI} and its power-tuned form PPI++ \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PPIEP} use the predictor on both samples to produce confidence intervals that remain valid for any predictor and are shorter than the labeled-only interval when the predictor is informative.\n120:\\paragraph{Pools and draws.} The sealed evaluation uses four real pools, each with $n=500$ labeled and $N=20000$ unlabeled records: California census income, census private-insurance indicator, a census education-related quantity (all with model predictions; census tabular benchmarks of the kind assembled by \\citealt{Ding2021RetiringAN}), and a plankton-imaging pool whose label and prediction are both binary. A fifth family is synthetic with four regimes (linear calibrated, nonlinear miscalibrated with a cubic predictor, uninformative, heteroscedastic), $n\\in\\{200,500\\}$ and $N\\in\\{2000,20000\\}$. Each family has 100 independent draws; real-pool draws are taken with replacement from the pool, so the truth is the pool mean, and synthetic truths are analytic. Controls with a constant predictor, a permuted predictor and a shifted outcome are run separately.\n277:Search pools: galaxy morphology (Galaxy Zoo-type crowd labels, \\citealt{Willett2013GalaxyZ2}; $n=300$, $N=5000$), Amazon deforestation ($n=100$, $N=1000$), gene expression ($n=500$, $N=10000$), protein disorder ($n=300$, $N=5000$), and synthetic regimes, 60 draws per family, reset seeds 1001--1005. The sealed evaluation used 680 episodes (500 main and 180 control) and took 1400 seconds of the 5400-second budget. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#real]] @evidence[[analysis/headroom_derived.json#synthetic_regimes]]\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility}\nThe derived quantities are produced by \\texttt{analysis/headroom.py} (reads the pool files, the evaluator's protocol constants and the sealed metrics, writes \\texttt{analysis/headroom\\_derived.json}) and the figures by \\texttt{analysis/make\\_figures.py}; both run in seconds on a CPU with NumPy, SciPy, scikit-learn and Matplotlib. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data. Table~\\ref{tab:final} and Figure~\\ref{fig:final} come from the sealed metrics plus the headroom file, Table~\\ref{tab:search} and Figure~\\ref{fig:search} from the search-pool metrics files, and Figure~\\ref{fig:partition} from the sealed per-partition scores. The sealed evaluation itself is the external evaluator \\texttt{evaluate\\_final.py} run once on the frozen estimator, so it cannot be regenerated with different draws here. The synthetic ceilings use a fixed-seed Monte Carlo of $2\\times10^6$ draws per regime.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nA large language model agent system was used throughout this study. It proposed the estimator designs, wrote and debugged the estimator code, ran the search-pool evaluations, wrote the headroom analysis and figure scripts, and drafted this manuscript. The task definition, the data pools and both evaluators were provided externally and were not modified; the sealed evaluation was run by the evaluator on the frozen estimator. All numbers in this paper come from the evaluator outputs or from the scripts cited above.\n\n\\end{document}\npaper_strategy.json:  \"central_thesis\": \"Adding a cross-fitted monotone/binned recalibration g(Yhat) as an extra control variate nested around PPI++ gives a small but measurable average narrowing of mean intervals (about 2% in geometric-mean width on held-out sealed pools, ~7% vs PPI lambda=1, ~30% vs classical), concentrated where the predictor is miscalibrated (census income, plankton, miscalibrated synthetic) and near zero where the raw prediction is already well calibrated, with empirical coverage held at a level the evaluator's exact binomial tests did not reject but whose point estimates (pooled 0.934, minimum family 0.91) sit below the nominal 0.95.\",\npaper_strategy.json:      \"name\": \"Cross-fitted recalibrated PPI (isotonic and quantile-binned g(Yhat); [Yhat, g] as control variates with variance-minimising weights; Student-t critical value; adopt the recalibrated interval only if its half-width is at least 1% below PPI++)\",\npaper_strategy.json:    \"Sealed final evaluation (5 families, each 100 independent labeled/unlabeled draws, n=500, N=20000 for the four real pools; synthetic mixes four regimes, n in {200,500}, N in {2000,20000}): per-family log width ratio vs PPI++ (census_income 0.0287, census_healthcare 0.0018, census_education 0.0023, plankton 0.0359, synthetic 0.0413), aggregate 0.0220; convert to width reductions (1-exp(-s)) as derived values and say so.\",\npaper_strategy.json:    \"Where gain exists vs not: gains largest on synthetic (nonlinear miscalibrated regime is the designed case), plankton and census_income; near zero on census_healthcare and census_education. Do not attribute causes beyond what the analytic argument and predictor-calibration reasoning support; no per-regime ablation was run, so mechanisms are hypotheses unless a derivation backs them.\",\npaper_strategy.json:    \"Search-stage design contrast on disjoint search pools (galaxies, forest, gene_expression, alphafold, synthetic; scored attempts 3 of 3, none unscored): winner 0.0271, stacked-calibration 0.0223, label-free basis 0.0046; per-family log ratios vs PPI++ for the winner: alphafold 0.0104, forest 0.0168, galaxies 0.0035, gene_expression 0.0290, synthetic 0.0757. Search coverage pooled 0.943 / min family 0.933 for the winner; the stacked variant had min family 0.917. Label the contrast as single evaluations; separate it clearly from final results.\",\npaper_strategy.json:        \"grounded_content\": \"Exact form of the frozen interval: PPI++ closed-form lambda clipped to [0,1]; 5-fold cross-fitted isotonic (direction by correlation sign) and quantile-binned g (bins = min(10, max(2, floor(0.8n/25)))); [Yhat, g] standardised control variates with ridge-stabilised variance-minimising weights; Student-t critical value with df = n-1-p via Cornish-Fisher; 1% half-width margin rule vs PPI++; classical fallback on non-finite output. Taken from the frozen program source.\",\npaper_strategy.json:          \"final.metric.log_ratio_vs_ppi_family_plankton\",\npaper_strategy.json:    \"No ablation artifacts exist (evidence_sources.ablations is missing). Describe the isotonic/binned/fallback components, but do not claim component-level attributions; state which questions remain open (effect of K, bins, margin, t vs normal, n sweep, small-n regime).\",\npaper_strategy.json:    \"Stratified PPI is in the frozen source but has no reported evaluator metrics; mention only as related/implemented baseline without numbers. Cite only keys present in references.bib (PPI Angelopoulos2023PredictionpoweredI, PPI++ Angelopoulos2023PPIEP, cross-PPI Zrnic2023CrosspredictionpoweredI, stratified PPI Fisch2024StratifiedPI, and the clearly relevant Gu2024LocalPI, Li2025PredictionPoweredAS, Cortinovis2025FABPPIFA, Kluger2025PredictionPoweredIW, Hofer2024BayesianPI where genuinely related); do not cite unrelated entries (credit risk, cardiac, microbiome, soil, conformal ICL). Double-machine-learning, isotonic and control-variate literature is not in the bib; cite only if retrieved later, otherwise describe without citation.\",\nclaims_grounding.json:   \"claim\": \"Per-family log ratios vs PPI++: income 0.0287, insurance 0.0018, education 0.0023, plankton 0.0359, synthetic 0.0413; derived reductions 2.8, 0.2, 0.2, 3.5, 4.0 percent\",\nclaims_grounding.json:    \"final.metric.log_ratio_vs_ppi_family_plankton\",\nclaims_grounding.json:   \"claim\": \"Population-level oracle ceilings vs PPI++ (derived): income 0.0213, insurance 0.0008, education 0.0009, plankton 0, synthetic 0.0416, aggregate 0.0129; unclipped-linear part for income 0.0179, aggregate 0.0036; observed exceeds ceiling in four of five families, aggregate excess 0.0091; ceilings vs PPI lambda=1 0.0661 and vs labeled-only 0.3498\",\nclaims_grounding.json:    \"final.metric.log_ratio_vs_ppi_family_plankton\",\nclaims_grounding.json:   \"claim\": \"Plankton predictor and label are binary so the ceiling is zero while observed narrowing is 0.0359\",\nclaims_grounding.json:    \"final.metric.log_ratio_vs_ppi_family_plankton\"\nclaims_grounding.json:    \"Willett2013GalaxyZ2\"\nclaims_grounding.json:   \"claim\": \"Search pools and sizes (galaxy n=300 N=5000, deforestation n=100 N=1000, gene expression n=500 N=10000, protein disorder n=300 N=5000, 60 draws per family, seeds 1001-1005); three variants each scored once\",\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c3f5a306-5336-48e1-ab3a-94ad94a1a36a","uuid":"46d99e59-65c5-4cbb-9fa4-0a8eeb3f8370","timestamp":"2026-11-11T12:02:55.077Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Willett2013 1\nLiu2024 0\nFannjiang 0\nHuang2025 0\nGan2022 0\nTran2025 0\nKhelfaoui 0\nZobeiri 0\n44:In ecological remote sensing, galaxy morphology and census analysis, a scientist often holds three things: a small set of gold-standard labels, a large set of records without labels, and a pretrained predictor that cannot be modified. Prediction-powered inference \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PredictionpoweredI} and its power-tuned form PPI++ \\citep{Angelopoulos2023PPIEP} use the predictor on both samples to produce confidence intervals that remain valid for any predictor and are shorter than the labeled-only interval when the predictor is informative.\n120:\\paragraph{Pools and draws.} The sealed evaluation uses four real pools, each with $n=500$ labeled and $N=20000$ unlabeled records: California census income, census private-insurance indicator, a census education-related quantity (all with model predictions; census tabular benchmarks of the kind assembled by \\citealt{Ding2021RetiringAN}), and a plankton-imaging pool whose label and prediction are both binary. A fifth family is synthetic w